---
name: parameterless-def-extension
description: "a parameterless extension member is invoked at its mention on the receiver"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `extension (s: String) def boxed: Box`

An extension member declared without a parameter clause takes exactly one thing — the receiver —
and mentioning it on a receiver is the call. All four lanes agree here.

This case was first written with a `known-red` for v2, on the strength of a probe that failed there.
The probe differed from this case in two ways at once — it used the single-line `extension (s) def m`
spelling and wrapped its call in `def main()` — and BOTH are needed to reproduce; neither has
anything to do with the missing parameter clause this case is about. The harness said so by failing
the declaration as STALE the first time it ran. That defect is `extension-call-in-a-def-body`.

```scalascript
case class Box(v: String)
extension (s: String)
  def boxed: Box = Box(s)
  def shout: String = s + "!"
println("L".boxed.v)
println("hi".shout)
```
